# proto-file: robot_runtime/robot_ipc/proto/network_config.proto
# proto-message: robot_ipc_proto.NetworkConfig
#
# An example network file for the hardware split (humanoid_nmpc/docs/distributed_runtime/README.md, "Deployment"):
# the robot process on the robot's computer, the MPC node, the GUI and the teleoperation on the laptop. Copy it, put the
# two machines' addresses in `host`, and give the same file to both sides:
#
#   make deploy-robot ROBOT=drc_atlas HOST=<robot> NETWORK=<this file>   # copied next to the robot's compose file
#   make launch-drc-atlas-mpc NETWORK=<this file>                         # on the laptop
#
# Every node binds every interface of its machine (bind_host "0.0.0.0"), so it is reachable whatever interface the link
# uses, and the other processes connect to its `host`. Each machine must accept TCP connections on the ports of its
# own nodes from the other one: the robot on 5600, the laptop on 5610-5622. The bus uses the range 5600-5629, e.g.
#
#   sudo ufw allow from <laptop address> to any port 5600:5629 proto tcp    # on the robot
#   sudo ufw allow from <robot address> to any port 5600:5629 proto tcp     # on the laptop
#
# There is no discovery: an address that changes (DHCP) needs a new file. Give the robot a static address or a DHCP
# reservation.

# LINT.IfChange(two_machine_nodes)
nodes { name: "robot"     host: "192.168.1.10"  port: 5600  bind_host: "0.0.0.0" }  # the robot's computer
nodes { name: "mpc"       host: "192.168.1.20"  port: 5610  bind_host: "0.0.0.0" }  # the laptop: the MPC node
nodes { name: "operator"  host: "192.168.1.20"  port: 5620  bind_host: "0.0.0.0" }  # the laptop: the remote_control GUI
nodes { name: "teleop"    host: "192.168.1.20"  port: 5621  bind_host: "0.0.0.0" }  # the laptop: teleoperation
nodes { name: "config_push"  host: "192.168.1.20"  port: 5622  bind_host: "0.0.0.0" }  # the laptop: push_robot_config
# LINT.ThenChange(//config/ipc/network.textproto:localhost_nodes)
